import { CollectionTransformCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> CollectionTransformCopy = CollectionTransformCopy {
  summary: "items.flatMap produces a List from each element, then joins those Lists by one level. It preserves input order and the order within each returned List.",
  taskTitle: "Collect each group’s tasks into one list",
  purpose: "Extract tasks from the Today, Waiting, and Tomorrow groups. Waiting has no tasks. Join the other tasks in order and display Read, Review, Ship.",
  typescriptBefore: "With normal arrays, groups.flatMap(group => group.tasks) extracts each object’s tasks and joins them by one level. An empty tasks array adds nothing. This compares the same task without treating JavaScript arrays and Seseragi Lists as the same data structure.",
  benefit: "Seseragi also accepts one function describing what to extract. It joins the elements of the returned List<String> values, so the result is one List<String> rather than List<List<String>>.",
  setup: "items is the alias for std/list. struct Group defines a record type with title: String and tasks: List<String>, and Group { ... } constructs a value. let groups names a List containing three Group records. A backtick before each opening [ in groups and its tasks creates a List.",
  after: [
    "\\group -> group.tasks is an unnamed function that receives one group and returns its tasks field. The \\ starts the argument and the expression after -> is the returned value. Pass this function first and groups second to items.flatMap.",
    "Today supplies `[\"Read\", \"Review\"], Waiting supplies `[], and Tomorrow supplies `[\"Ship\"]. The results are joined in that order. Waiting is still passed to the function, but its empty returned List adds no elements.",
    "The comprehension [value | value <- ...] reads each element of the flatMap result as value and creates a display Array. These brackets have no backtick, but flatMap itself returns a List. The JSON output is [\"Read\",\"Review\",\"Ship\"]; the groups’ title fields are not included.",
  ],
  details: [
    "The function is applied once to each Group from the beginning. The elements of each result keep their order, and neither groups nor its tasks are changed. An empty groups collection calls no function and returns an empty List. This example’s function only reads a field and performs no external operations.",
    "Only one level of each returned List is joined. If two inputs produce `[`[1], `[11]] and `[`[2], `[12]], the result is `[`[1], `[11], `[2], `[12]]. The deeper Lists remain intact.",
    "The function must return a List. Returning only the String group.title does not fit. Return `[group.title] to contribute one title. Without the backtick, [group.title] is an Array and does not fit this List operation.",
    "Use concat when you already have List<List<String>> and need no per-element transformation. Use filterMap when each input contributes at most one value and you want to express absence with Nothing.",
  ],
}
